# Every 20 minutes, 07:30–21:50 in the owner's zone (the box runs UTC; the
# zone is named so daylight saving moves the window with the owner, not
# against them).
#
# **Not 07:00.** That is the `morning` briefing trigger's minute, in the same
# zone (see mecha-mail-classify.timer), and the idle check only protects the
# briefing if the briefing reaches a model slot first — a coin flip. The
# 07:00 tick would also carry the whole night's mail, the day's most
# expensive sweep, into the briefing's window. Half an hour later costs
# nothing: the briefing is where that mail gets read anyway.
#
# **Not on the hour either.** `mecha-frontdoor.timer` is `hourly` with a
# 5-minute spread, and its extract and triage legs are model-bound with no
# idle check of their own — the 07:00 argument, fourteen more times a day.
# :10, :30 and :50 stay clear of its whole window.
#
# `Persistent=` is deliberately off, unlike the nightly's: a missed daytime
# tick is covered by the next one twenty minutes later, and replaying a
# morning's worth of ticks after a reboot would be a burst of exactly the
# load this unit exists to avoid. A oneshot that is still running when the
# next tick comes is not started twice — systemd skips it — so a slow sweep
# cannot pile up behind itself.
[Unit]
Description=Classify new mail through the working day

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 07:30/20:00 America/New_York
OnCalendar=*-*-* 08..21:10/20:00 America/New_York
RandomizedDelaySec=60
AccuracySec=1min

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
